<Rive version="1" kind="fragment">
    <!--
      The streak celebration's flame: a book lying with its spine running away from the
      viewer, falling open, with a flame rising out of the gutter.

      ORIENTATION. The spine is along Z, so the camera sits at the book's TAIL edge looking
      along the spine. We see the two halves splaying left and right, the page block between
      them, the stacked page edges along the near edge, and the gutter as a dark seam up the
      middle. The flame rises along +Y out of that seam. Rive is 2D, so the depth is drawn
      rather than transformed: the far edge of every quad is narrower and higher than its
      near edge, converging up-screen.

      WHAT OPENING IS, HERE. The first draft of this file rotated the two halves apart, and
      that is wrong for this camera -- a 2D rotation pivots about a point, and the inner edge
      of a half is not a point but the *gutter line*, which recedes. Rotating a half swung
      its far-inner corner clean across the centre line, so the two halves crossed into a
      bow tie. Opening is therefore **scale**, anchored at the gutter: a shut book seen down
      its spine is one board wide, an open one is nearly two, so `scaleX` runs 0.54 -> 1.0
      and the inner edge stays pinned where it belongs. `scaleY` flattens the shallow V of
      the open book back into the slab of a closed one.

      The covers are what a shut book shows and the page surfaces are what an open one shows,
      and **the page surfaces arrive by unfolding rather than by fading.** `page_*` was keyed
      on opacity at first, which is a cross-dissolve: for the whole of the first fifth of the
      sequence a half-transparent page sat over a near-black cover and the book read as a grey
      slab -- and since the opening happens in that same window, most of it happened in the
      mud. It took a sheet of the *in-betweens* to see, because both ends of that blend are
      correct and only the middle is wrong. `scaleY` 0 -> 1 instead, anchored at the tail
      edge, which is also what the object does: a page surface is edge-on when the book is
      shut and rotates into view as it opens, so it foreshortens to nothing rather than
      becoming transparent. Every frame stays opaque.

      THE CONTRACT. This artboard does not play itself. `Flame/progress` is a Number in
      0-100, written every frame by Flutter's own AnimationController
      (`lib/ui/widgets/streak/streak_flame.dart`), and the blend state below poses the scene
      at whatever it says. That is deliberate and it is what makes Rive acceptable here:
      the celebration's beats stay in Dart where they are greppable and a golden test can
      drive them, `MediaQuery.disableAnimationsOf` needs no special case (the controller
      jumps to 1, progress becomes 100, the scene poses at its final frame), and pose 100 is
      a RESTING pose -- nothing moves once the last beat lands, on a screen a reader opens
      nightly.

      So: no `loopValue`, no idle wobble, no autoplaying timeline. Every animation below is
      a single-frame POSE, and the blend between them is the animation.

      COLOURS are shipped tokens, not new ones. `library_card/card_lighting.dart` and
      `app_theme.dart` own them; this file has picked none of its own, because inventing
      brand colours is a mistake this repo has already made once (see the first comment
      block of `docs/mockups/streaks/index.html`).
        FFFFD479  flame core     - the record's lit-today highlight
        FFF2A93F  flame body     - kCandleFlame
        FFB54708  flame edge     - AppColors.light.flame
        FF26190A  gutter seam    - kCandleStockTop
        FF35230E  cover          - kCandleWellTop
        FF1B1106  cover, near    - kCandleWellBottom
        FFFDFAF1  page, lit      - the record's paper
        FFFFE8C4  page, falling  - kCandleGlow
        FFD9A96B  page, far      - kCandleCoverLight
        FFE2D9C4  page edges     - the record's hairline

      The ground this sits on is kCandleGlow #FFE8C4, painted by `streak_celebration.dart`.
      A *lighter* halo is invisible on it; the glow below is therefore warmer rather than
      brighter, and the page gradient runs paper -> cream -> tan outward from the flame so
      the light has somewhere to fall off to.
    -->
    <Artboard defaultStateMachineId="0:7" styleId="0:5" viewModelId="0:100"
              viewModelInstanceId="0:102" width="304" height="304" name="Flame" id="0:2">
        <LayoutComponentStyle name="Artboard Style" id="0:5"/>

        <!-- No background Fill. The celebration paints its own cream ground, and a filled
             artboard would sit on it as a visible panel.

             `ground` below is the exception that proves it, and it is deliberate. A
             `rive --screenshot` composites onto the CLI's own opaque #1D1D1D canvas and
             there is no flag to change that: a `kind="fragment"` document may not declare a
             `<Backboard>`, and `rive.yaml`'s `artboard.background` applies only to projects
             with no RML. So every semi-transparent thing here -- the glow above all -- came
             back from the previewer darkened toward black, and `sheet.py`'s chroma key
             cannot recover an alpha it was never given: the first honest-looking sheet
             showed the glow as a solid black disc that does not exist. Rather than judge the
             light through a lie, the artboard carries a cream rectangle bound to a second
             Number that **defaults to 0 and is switched on only by `sheet.py`**. Flutter
             never writes it, so the shipped artboard is transparent exactly as before. -->

        <!-- Draw order is declaration order, FRONT FIRST. So: the fire, then the seam it
             comes out of, then the two halves of the book, then the light behind all of it.
             **The first draft had the pages declared after the covers and so behind them**,
             which is why the book read as a dark mountain with a cream sliver on top. -->

        <!-- The fire, as one group. A single node carries the rise and the overall size
             ramp, so the poses say that once instead of once per shape; the shapes inside
             carry only what differs between them, which is the squash and stretch. -->
        <!-- The fire, as one group. A single node carries the rise and the overall size
             ramp, so the poses say that once instead of once per shape; the shapes inside
             carry only what differs between them, which is the squash and stretch.

             **It sits at the crease's mid-depth, not at its near end, and that is what makes
             the flame come *out of* the book.** Placed at the tail edge the flame's base sat
             in front of the whole page block and read as a spike stabbing down through it;
             seated a third of the way along the gutter it rises from the valley with the
             pages spread below it on both sides. -->
        <Node x="152" y="214" name="fire" id="0:20">

            <!-- Embers. Also a group, so the burst is one transform rather than forty
                 keyframes: the node scales outward while it fades. A **fan**, denser near
                 the flame and spreading up and out, and nothing below the book line -- an
                 even ring is what this looked like the first time and it read as a halo.

                 **Only the two bright tokens, and never the deep one.** Half of these are
                 in front of the flame's own body, and an ember painted `flame` #B54708 on a
                 body that runs to #B54708 at the base is an ember nobody can see -- the
                 first fan looked left-weighted for exactly that reason, with every
                 right-hand spark camouflaged against the flame it had just left. -->
            <Node y="-16" name="sparks" id="0:21">
                <Shape x="-13" y="-76" id="0:104"><Ellipse width="9" height="9" name="Path" id="0:105"/>
                    <Fill name="Fill" id="0:106"><SolidColor colorValue="FFFFD479" name="C" id="0:107"/></Fill></Shape>
                <Shape x="22" y="-88" id="0:108"><Ellipse width="8" height="8" name="Path" id="0:109"/>
                    <Fill name="Fill" id="0:110"><SolidColor colorValue="FFFFD479" name="C" id="0:111"/></Fill></Shape>
                <Shape x="-36" y="-50" id="0:112"><Ellipse width="8" height="8" name="Path" id="0:113"/>
                    <Fill name="Fill" id="0:114"><SolidColor colorValue="FFF2A93F" name="C" id="0:115"/></Fill></Shape>
                <Shape x="42" y="-42" id="0:116"><Ellipse width="7" height="7" name="Path" id="0:117"/>
                    <Fill name="Fill" id="0:118"><SolidColor colorValue="FFF2A93F" name="C" id="0:119"/></Fill></Shape>
                <Shape x="-50" y="-24" id="0:120"><Ellipse width="6" height="6" name="Path" id="0:121"/>
                    <Fill name="Fill" id="0:122"><SolidColor colorValue="FFF2A93F" name="C" id="0:123"/></Fill></Shape>
                <Shape x="8" y="-104" id="0:124"><Ellipse width="8" height="8" name="Path" id="0:125"/>
                    <Fill name="Fill" id="0:126"><SolidColor colorValue="FFFFD479" name="C" id="0:127"/></Fill></Shape>
                <Shape x="-25" y="-99" id="0:128"><Ellipse width="6" height="6" name="Path" id="0:129"/>
                    <Fill name="Fill" id="0:130"><SolidColor colorValue="FFFFD479" name="C" id="0:131"/></Fill></Shape>
                <Shape x="54" y="-68" id="0:132"><Ellipse width="6" height="6" name="Path" id="0:133"/>
                    <Fill name="Fill" id="0:134"><SolidColor colorValue="FFFFD479" name="C" id="0:135"/></Fill></Shape>
                <Shape x="-60" y="-60" id="0:136"><Ellipse width="5" height="5" name="Path" id="0:137"/>
                    <Fill name="Fill" id="0:138"><SolidColor colorValue="FFF2A93F" name="C" id="0:139"/></Fill></Shape>
                <Shape x="37" y="-14" id="0:140"><Ellipse width="5" height="5" name="Path" id="0:141"/>
                    <Fill name="Fill" id="0:142"><SolidColor colorValue="FFFFD479" name="C" id="0:143"/></Fill></Shape>
            </Node>

            <!-- The shimmer sweep: a soft bright bar that slides across the flame. Clipped
                 to the flame's own silhouette, because the whole point is that the flame
                 gleams and not that a white stripe crosses the screen. The hand-built
                 version's gleam was wide enough to turn the flame to paper; this one is
                 narrow, soft at both edges, and gone by rest. -->
            <Shape y="-55" rotation="-0.45" name="gleam" id="0:23">
                <PointsPath isClosed="true" isClockwise="true" name="Path" id="0:144">
                    <StraightVertex x="-11" y="-110" id="0:145"/>
                    <StraightVertex x="11" y="-110" id="0:146"/>
                    <StraightVertex x="11" y="110" id="0:147"/>
                    <StraightVertex x="-11" y="110" id="0:148"/>
                </PointsPath>
                <Fill name="Fill" id="0:149">
                    <LinearGradient startX="-11" startY="0" endX="11" endY="0" name="Gradient" id="0:150">
                        <GradientStop colorValue="00FDFAF1" position="0" id="0:151"/>
                        <GradientStop colorValue="6BFDFAF1" position="0.5" id="0:152"/>
                        <GradientStop colorValue="00FDFAF1" position="1" id="0:153"/>
                    </LinearGradient>
                </Fill>
                <ClippingShape sourceId="0:25" name="Clip" id="0:154"/>
            </Shape>

            <!-- The bright core. Its own shape rather than a lighter fill, so it can lick
                 independently of the body -- `rotation` on this and not on the body is the
                 flame moving rather than the flame tilting. -->
            <Shape y="3" name="flame_inner" id="0:24">
                <PointsPath isClosed="true" isClockwise="true" name="Path" id="0:155">
                    <StraightVertex x="3" y="-78" id="0:156"/>
                    <CubicMirroredVertex x="20" y="-42" rotation="1.5707964" distance="24" id="0:157"/>
                    <CubicMirroredVertex x="13" y="-12" rotation="2.5" distance="14" id="0:158"/>
                    <CubicMirroredVertex x="0" y="5" rotation="3.1415927" distance="13" id="0:159"/>
                    <CubicMirroredVertex x="-14" y="-14" rotation="3.9" distance="14" id="0:160"/>
                    <CubicMirroredVertex x="-18" y="-45" rotation="4.85" distance="24" id="0:161"/>
                </PointsPath>
                <Fill name="Fill" id="0:162">
                    <LinearGradient startX="0" startY="-78" endX="0" endY="3" name="Gradient" id="0:163">
                        <GradientStop colorValue="FFFFD479" position="0" id="0:164"/>
                        <GradientStop colorValue="FFFFD479" position="0.6" id="0:165"/>
                        <GradientStop colorValue="FFF2A93F" position="1" id="0:166"/>
                    </LinearGradient>
                </Fill>
            </Shape>

            <!-- The body. **A sharp apex, a narrow base, and an asymmetric belly; all three
                 are the point.** The first draft used four mirrored vertices and got an egg:
                 a flame is not an ellipse, it has a tip. The second got a tulip, because the
                 widest point sat too low and the base was as wide as the shoulders, so the
                 silhouette flared, pinched to a waist and flared again. A flame is widest
                 about a third of the way up and tapers to almost nothing where it meets what
                 is burning. The apex is a `StraightVertex` so the two curves meet in a corner
                 instead of rounding over, it sits right of centre so the silhouette leans,
                 and the left and right bellies differ. Gradient-filled, because a flat fill
                 is what made the hand-built version read as a sticker. -->
            <Shape name="flame_outer" id="0:25">
                <PointsPath isClosed="true" isClockwise="true" name="Path" id="0:167">
                    <StraightVertex x="6" y="-120" id="0:168"/>
                    <CubicMirroredVertex x="36" y="-64" rotation="1.5707964" distance="38" id="0:169"/>
                    <CubicMirroredVertex x="24" y="-18" rotation="2.5" distance="24" id="0:170"/>
                    <CubicMirroredVertex x="0" y="8" rotation="3.1415927" distance="22" id="0:171"/>
                    <CubicMirroredVertex x="-25" y="-20" rotation="3.9" distance="24" id="0:172"/>
                    <CubicMirroredVertex x="-32" y="-69" rotation="4.85" distance="38" id="0:173"/>
                </PointsPath>
                <Fill name="Fill" id="0:174">
                    <LinearGradient startX="0" startY="-120" endX="0" endY="6" name="Gradient" id="0:175">
                        <GradientStop colorValue="FFF2A93F" position="0" id="0:176"/>
                        <GradientStop colorValue="FFB54708" position="1" id="0:177"/>
                    </LinearGradient>
                </Fill>
            </Shape>
        </Node>

        <!-- The crease the flame comes out of. Declared before the two halves so it sits on
             top of their join, and faded out when the book is shut because a shut book has no
             visible gutter.

             **It stops exactly where the flame's base begins, and its whole job is pose 20.**
             Twice now a gutter drawn past that point has read as a black peg stood on the
             page: once as a blunt rectangle, once as a wedge. Below the flame the crease is
             carried by the page gradient's own shadow instead, which is where a crease comes
             from anyway. What is left here is the seam that appears in the moment after the
             covers fall open and before there is a flame big enough to hide it. -->
        <Shape x="152" y="238" name="gutter" id="0:26">
            <PointsPath isClosed="true" isClockwise="true" name="Path" id="0:178">
                <StraightVertex x="-6" y="-16" id="0:179"/>
                <StraightVertex x="6" y="-16" id="0:180"/>
                <StraightVertex x="2" y="-60" id="0:181"/>
                <StraightVertex x="-2" y="-60" id="0:182"/>
            </PointsPath>
            <Fill name="Fill" id="0:183"><SolidColor colorValue="FF26190A" name="Color" id="0:184"/></Fill>
        </Shape>

        <!-- The two halves. Each is a Node hinged at the gutter's near end, holding three
             shapes: the stacked page edges we are looking straight at, the page surface
             receding away, and the cover board behind both, showing as a rim.

             Written out twice rather than mirrored with `scaleX="-1"`, because the opening
             *is* `scaleX` and a right half whose open pose reads `-1.0` is a trap for
             whoever edits the pose table next. -->
        <Node x="152" y="238" name="wing_left" id="0:27">
            <!-- The tail edge: the band between the page block's near edge and the cover's.
                 **This is the shape that says we are looking at the bottom of a book** and
                 not at two flat wings -- it is the only element that gives the thing
                 thickness. -->
            <Shape name="tail_left" id="0:185">
                <PointsPath isClosed="true" isClockwise="true" name="Path" id="0:186">
                    <StraightVertex x="0" y="11" id="0:187"/>
                    <StraightVertex x="-105" y="-3" id="0:188"/>
                    <StraightVertex x="-116" y="4" id="0:189"/>
                    <StraightVertex x="0" y="20" id="0:190"/>
                </PointsPath>
                <Fill name="Fill" id="0:191"><SolidColor colorValue="FFE2D9C4" name="Color" id="0:192"/></Fill>
            </Shape>
            <Shape name="page_left" id="0:28">
                <PointsPath isClosed="true" isClockwise="true" name="Path" id="0:193">
                    <StraightVertex x="0" y="11" radius="2" id="0:194"/>
                    <StraightVertex x="-105" y="-3" radius="2" id="0:195"/>
                    <StraightVertex x="-65" y="-53" radius="2" id="0:196"/>
                    <StraightVertex x="0" y="-58" radius="2" id="0:197"/>
                </PointsPath>
                <Fill name="Fill" id="0:198">
                    <!-- **The crease is the darkest part of an open book, not the
                         brightest.** The first version ran paper-bright at x=0 outward on
                         the reasoning that the flame is the light source and the gutter is
                         nearest to it -- and drew a pale needle straight down the middle of
                         the book, because both halves put their brightest pixel against x=0
                         and nothing covered the join below the flame. Two pages meeting in a
                         valley shade each other, which is why a real open book has a dark
                         line down it; so the crease gets the shadow tone, the lit band sits
                         just outside it, and the falloff still runs outward from there. -->
                    <LinearGradient startX="0" startY="-23" endX="-105" endY="-27" name="Gradient" id="0:199">
                        <GradientStop colorValue="FFD9A96B" position="0" id="0:200"/>
                        <GradientStop colorValue="FFD9A96B" position="0.03" id="0:201"/>
                        <GradientStop colorValue="FFFDFAF1" position="0.11" id="0:202"/>
                        <GradientStop colorValue="FFFFE8C4" position="1" id="0:203"/>
                    </LinearGradient>
                </Fill>
                <!-- No Stroke. **A hairline around this path puts one up the crease**, and
                     the left and right halves' two strokes meeting at x=0 drew a pale needle
                     from the flame's base down to the tail edge -- the page block's edge is
                     already carried by `tail_left` below and the cover's rim outside it, so
                     the outline was doing nothing the drawing needed and one thing it did
                     not. `PointsPath` strokes every edge or none. -->
            </Shape>
            <Shape name="cover_left" id="0:204">
                <PointsPath isClosed="true" isClockwise="true" name="Path" id="0:205">
                    <StraightVertex x="0" y="20" radius="3" id="0:206"/>
                    <StraightVertex x="-116" y="4" radius="3" id="0:207"/>
                    <StraightVertex x="-72" y="-58" radius="3" id="0:208"/>
                    <StraightVertex x="0" y="-64" radius="3" id="0:209"/>
                </PointsPath>
                <Fill name="Fill" id="0:210">
                    <LinearGradient startX="0" startY="-64" endX="0" endY="20" name="Gradient" id="0:211">
                        <GradientStop colorValue="FF35230E" position="0" id="0:212"/>
                        <GradientStop colorValue="FF1B1106" position="1" id="0:213"/>
                    </LinearGradient>
                </Fill>
            </Shape>
        </Node>

        <Node x="152" y="238" name="wing_right" id="0:29">
            <Shape name="tail_right" id="0:214">
                <PointsPath isClosed="true" isClockwise="false" name="Path" id="0:215">
                    <StraightVertex x="0" y="11" id="0:216"/>
                    <StraightVertex x="105" y="-3" id="0:217"/>
                    <StraightVertex x="116" y="4" id="0:218"/>
                    <StraightVertex x="0" y="20" id="0:219"/>
                </PointsPath>
                <Fill name="Fill" id="0:220"><SolidColor colorValue="FFE2D9C4" name="Color" id="0:221"/></Fill>
            </Shape>
            <Shape name="page_right" id="0:31">
                <PointsPath isClosed="true" isClockwise="false" name="Path" id="0:222">
                    <StraightVertex x="0" y="11" radius="2" id="0:223"/>
                    <StraightVertex x="105" y="-3" radius="2" id="0:224"/>
                    <StraightVertex x="65" y="-53" radius="2" id="0:225"/>
                    <StraightVertex x="0" y="-58" radius="2" id="0:226"/>
                </PointsPath>
                <Fill name="Fill" id="0:227">
                    <LinearGradient startX="0" startY="-23" endX="105" endY="-27" name="Gradient" id="0:228">
                        <GradientStop colorValue="FFD9A96B" position="0" id="0:229"/>
                        <GradientStop colorValue="FFD9A96B" position="0.03" id="0:230"/>
                        <GradientStop colorValue="FFFDFAF1" position="0.11" id="0:231"/>
                        <GradientStop colorValue="FFFFE8C4" position="1" id="0:232"/>
                    </LinearGradient>
                </Fill>
            </Shape>
            <Shape name="cover_right" id="0:233">
                <PointsPath isClosed="true" isClockwise="false" name="Path" id="0:234">
